1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,370
Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession.
在美国，工程长期以来一直是男性主导的职业。

2
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:09,370
Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.
五十年前，这一局面看起来有可能改变。

3
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:16,120
In 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%.
1970年，主修工程的女性所占比例不到1%。

4
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:20,480
In 1979, that number was 9%.
1979年，这一数字为9%。

5
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:25,130
Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate.
许多人希望女性会继续以同样的速度进入这一领域。

6
00:00:25,930 --> 00:00:27,430
But that’s not what happened.
但事情并没有这样发展。

7
00:00:28,230 --> 00:00:34,890
Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.
如今，工程专业的学生中只有21%是女性，这一数字自2000年以来基本没有变化。

8
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:44,900
I am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s.
我是一名历史学家，曾与同事一道调查了251位20世纪70年代大学毕业的女工程师。

9
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:51,650
These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.
这些先驱回顾了她们当年面临的种种挑战，也为如今进入这一领域的女性提出了建议。

10
00:00:52,450 --> 00:01:09,720
One survey taker explained, “The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.”
一位受访者解释说：“对我来说，最大的挑战是坚持相信自己，可当时我得到的所有信息都在告诉我：我永远不会被认真对待，也不会和男性以同样的速度获得晋升或加薪，而那些男性显然资质不如我，也没有我聪明。”

11
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:16,830
A chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, “You have to prove yourself just because you are female.
一位在制造业工作的化学工程师也表示赞同：“就因为你是女性，你就得证明自己。

12
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:19,560
And you have to work twice as hard!”
而且你还得加倍努力！”

13
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:23,570
A civil engineer said, “We are ‘women engineers.’
一位土木工程师说：“我们是‘女工程师’。

14
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:28,680
People don’t refer to a man as a ‘man engineer’—he’s an engineer.
人们不会把男性称为‘男工程师’——他就是工程师。

15
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,490
We are constantly reminded that we don’t truly belong.”
我们不断被提醒：自己并不真正属于这里。”

16
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:38,660
Another civil engineer stated, “On many levels, you’re never quite one of the group.”
另一位土木工程师表示：“从很多层面来说，你始终不完全算是这个群体中的一员。”

17
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,190
Women also talked about family caregiving responsibilities.
这些女性还谈到了照料家人的责任。

18
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:59,140
A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, “Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children; then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don’t have the same household responsibilities.”
一家大型化工公司的退休副总裁表示：“年轻女工程师在生孩子之前都处于平等的地位；有了孩子之后，她们就要艰难地兼顾工作与家庭——还要与那些没有同样家庭责任的男性竞争。”

19
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:02,110
But over the years things have changed a lot.
但多年来，情况已经发生了很大变化。

20
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:07,550
Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.
年轻女工程师更受接纳，主要就是因为她们的人数多了。

21
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:11,960
Many women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career.
许多女工程师都盛赞了自己所选职业的种种好处。

22
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:17,640
A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree.
一位在制造业工作的项目经理表示，工程学位是最好的学位。

23
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:23,540
A mechanical engineer said, “It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything.
一位机械工程师说：“它会给你灵活选择的余地，让你几乎什么事都能做。

24
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,730
It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.”
看到自己所做的事产生效果，也让人很满足。”
